(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112200-free-sites-namespace")
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/title "Free user sites use sites.itonami.app; identity and storage retain their existing boundaries")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/topic "free-sites")
  (:adr/last_verified "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/body "# Free sites under sites.itonami.app\n\nDecision accepted by the owner on 2026-09-11 in the FreeDomain planning task.\n\nThe user-facing free hostname is {name}.sites.itonami.app. The existing\n{name}.itonami.app namespace remains the first-party app entry. kotoba.cloud\nowns registration, identity and publication approval; kotobase.net owns durable\ncontent, signed publication history and storage grants; itonami.cloud owns work.\nThe root public-sites policy is a destination contract, not a live registration\nservice. General registration is NOT open and no PSL acceptance is claimed.\n\nIdentity stays the CID, with an IPNS key where its ownership and update protocol\nare qualified. DNSLink is the naming projection. The currently qualified named\nHTTP path accepts a bare /ipfs/{cid}; /ipns and subpath targets are not promised.\nStatic self-contained documents are the first release. Directory sites require\nan explicit extension of the existing single-document contract and UnixFS tests.\n\nWhy this boundary\n\nkotoba.cloud currently uses a Domain=kotoba.cloud authentication cookie. Public\nHTML, DNS delegation, previews and uploads must not be placed inside that domain.\nThe sites label alone is not a browser security boundary. Before public tenant\nscripts are enabled, qualify the PSL boundary, host-only cookies, Origin and\nCSRF enforcement, CORS, redirect handoffs and WebAuthn RP restrictions. A PSL\napplication is an external review, not something a deployment can self-approve.\nFirst-party apps must not broaden their session cookies to itonami.app.\nNo free-zone NS delegation or email service is included. Moving to unrestricted\nDNS hosting requires a separate risk and domain decision.\n\nRequired registration and publication contract\n\n1. Authenticate a Stable Principal at kotoba.cloud with the existing viewer.\n   Derive owner identity from the verified session, never a submitted owner ID.\n   Require same-origin mutation, bounded bodies and the existing publication\n   cryptographic floor; do not create a weaker public-upload signing path.\n2. Normalize the name to one lowercase ASCII DNS label, reject IDN/punycode in\n   the initial release, reserve operational/brand names and reject dots/slashes.\n3. Atomically claim one name for one Principal and enforce one free site per\n   Principal in the SAME serialized claim operation. Never implement ownership\n   with an eventually consistent DNS or KV read followed by a write. Persistent\n   authority lives at the Kotobase boundary; DNS is only its projection.\n4. Save and verify content before changing the public name. Reuse the canonical\n   publication path writing archive and web stores; do not publish arbitrary\n   caller CIDs merely because bytes exist in the shared bucket. Require owner\n   custody, explicit public release and publication authorization.\n5. An immutable signed publication record binds owner, name, content CID,\n   previous record CID, monotonic sequence and policy version. DNS changes are\n   retryable jobs with an expected sequence. A stale retry cannot overwrite a\n   newer publication. Never report published until DNS and HTTPS readback agree.\n6. Export content and history without requiring a paid upgrade. A site's name\n   remains dependent on our registered domain. Content can move independently.\n7. Suspension stops our serving/naming and retries of pending publication jobs.\n   Keep a durable tombstone; do not immediately reassign a released name. Data\n   already copied outside our systems cannot be recalled by deleting our pin.\n\nPilot product limits (proposed limits, not current entitlements)\n\nOne site, 100 MB total retained bytes including history, latest plus up to ten\nprevious versions subject to the same byte cap, and 1 GB/month transfer.\nNo automatic paid conversion. Enforce quota at the serving/upload boundaries,\nincluding concurrent requests; measure actual storage and support costs before\nopening broadly. Existing Kotobase grants remain authoritative until the pilot\nentitlement is implemented. Static templates and explicit publish are the UI.\n\nDeployment acceptance\n\n- DNS for *.sites.itonami.app and _dnslink.{name}.sites.itonami.app is scoped;\n  require valid TLS for the extra label, not an assumed *.itonami.app certificate.\n- Unregistered names return 404 and never a tenant or first-party fallback.\n- Immutable CID URLs retain long caching plus no-transform. Mutable DNSLink\n  names use a short TTL and no-transform, including 304 and range responses.\n- Independent SHA-256 verification of a raw-CID site must match at the archive,\n  CID web URL and named URL. HTML/analytics transformations invalidate the\n  receipt. HTTP 200 is insufficient.\n- Concurrent claims have one winner. Another Principal cannot publish, delete,\n  export private drafts or claim the winner's name. Suspended names stay stopped.\n- Failed storage/DNS writes preserve the last good public version; a retry is\n  idempotent. Test recovery and name changes, not only the initial upload.\n- Public opening requires enforced quotas, abuse intake/appeal, retention and\n  recovery operations, browser isolation, certificate renewal and measured costs.\n\nObservations on 2026-09-11\n\nGenko's DNSLink pointed to\nbafkreiffmgvpdzs6aoidrznnvyn2yxts2smhr7ghryj2yurozdjqlv4qve.\nThe archive returned 633269 bytes and the matching raw-CID SHA-256\n a561aaf1e65e039038e5adae1bac5e72d49878fcc78e13ac522ec8d305d790a9.\nThe web CID and named entries inserted Cloudflare analytics scripts. The origin\nsource also applied immutable one-year caching after DNSLink resolution. Fix\nboth in net-kotobase/ipfs before using that route for mutable user publications.\nThese observations are not a current multi-provider availability guarantee.\n\nRollout sequence\n\nA. Land this naming decision and qualify byte-preserving, mutable-name serving.\nB. Integrate atomic claims, owner custody, publication authorization, quotas,\n   versioned DNS projection and the existing control UI in an isolated pilot.\nC. Qualify TLS and browser boundaries; prepare the PSL submission with ownership\n   evidence, policy and an operating service. External acceptance remains pending.\nD. Run a small invited pilot, measure outcomes and cost, then evaluate opening.\n\nSuccess measures are first useful publication, third-party use, retained use,\nexport/restore success, and sustainable cost, not merely registered names.\n")
))
